# CMA-ES chains

Local-search chains combine a steady-state genetic algorithm with local
refinement that remembers its previous work. Each promising individual can
retain a complete local solver and state. Selecting it again extends the
same search instead of rebuilding its model from the population point.

`MaLsChCma` specializes the framework with CMA-ES. Each saved chain
retains its covariance, step size, evolution paths, and random generator.

[API reference](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/ma_ls_ch/struct.MaLsCh.html)

| Property | Requirement |
| --- | --- |
| Objective | A scalar function with useful local refinement |
| Evaluations | `CostFunction`, plus the inner operator's requirements |
| Search | Persistent local-search chains within a steady-state GA |
| Constraints | SamplingBounds on an unconstrained objective |
| Parameters | All four dense backends with a compatible inner |

## When to use it

Use chains when local refinement keeps improving a basin and its learned
search behavior is worth retaining. The framework follows
[Molina et al. (2010)](#ref-molina-et-al-2010). Compare
[CMA injection](../cma-inject/) or [DE injection](../de-inject/) when
independent short local solves are sufficient.

CMA chains learn correlations but store a dense covariance for each retained
individual. Solis–Wets chains use vector operations and linear storage,
making them a useful alternative as dimension grows. Neither configuration
certifies global optimality in a finite run.

## How it works

Each outer iteration first spends `nfrec` objective evaluations generating
SSGA offspring through negative assortative mating, BLX crossover, BGA
mutation, and replacement of the worst member. Replacing a member discards
its old chain.

The solver then selects the best eligible member. A member is eligible if
it has never received local search or its previous segment improved by at
least `with_ls_improvement_threshold` (default `1e-8`). If none qualifies,
the best population member is selected anyway.

A new chain starts with a scale equal to half the nearest-neighbor distance.
Zero or non-finite distances use `with_initial_scale_fallback`, default
`1.0`. A retained chain resumes without initialization. Each segment gets
its own cost budget, and an improved best evaluated point replaces the
population member. The chain survives only if its improvement meets the
threshold; otherwise, a later selection starts a fresh chain.

## Example

The sphere has its minimum at the origin, with cost zero. This example
uses a fixed seed and checks the historical best against that analytic
reference. A successful result on this objective does not certify global
optimality on a general problem.

<!-- compile: solver-ma-ls-ch-cma -->
```rust
use basin::{
    BoxBounds, Constrained, ConstraintSet, CostFunction, DenseMatrix, Executor,
    MaLsChCma, PopulationProgress, SamplingDomain,
};
use std::convert::Infallible;

struct Sphere;

impl CostFunction for Sphere {
    type Param = Vec<f64>;
    type Output = f64;
    type Error = Infallible;

    fn cost(&self, x: &Self::Param) -> Result<f64, Self::Error> {
        Ok(x.iter().map(|v| v * v).sum())
    }
}

fn main() {
    let bounds = BoxBounds::new(Some(vec![-5.0; 2]), Some(vec![5.0; 2]));
    let problem =
        Constrained::new(Sphere, ConstraintSet::new().with_bounds(bounds));
    let solver = MaLsChCma::<Vec<f64>, DenseMatrix>::new(7)
        .with_pop_size(20)
        .with_ls_intensity(100);

    let result = Executor::new(
        SamplingDomain::new(problem),
        solver,
        PopulationProgress::<Vec<f64>>::empty(),
    )
    .max_cost_evals(10_000)
    .run()
    .unwrap();

    assert!(result.best_cost() < 1e-4);
    assert!(result.best_param().iter().all(|v| v.abs() < 0.01));
}
```

## Practical considerations

### Distinguish sampling bounds from constraints

The objective must support `SamplingBounds` and be unconstrained. The
example explicitly reinterprets a box-only objective with `SamplingDomain`.
This removes its mathematical constraints: local searches and accepted
results may leave the sampling box. The objective must remain valid there.
Use bounded injection instead when the returned result must satisfy a box.

Sampling endpoints must be finite, ordered, and dimensionally consistent.
Empty progress samples the initial population; explicit progress from
`PopulationProgress::from_population` must match the configured population
size and dimension. Fresh initialization projects explicit members into the
sampling box and reevaluates them.

### Balance global exploration and local refinement

The default population has `60` members. The default local intensity is
`300` cost evaluations, and `nfrec` follows that intensity unless explicitly
set with `with_nfrec`. Raising the local share can refine basins more deeply
but leaves less budget to discover alternatives. The improvement threshold
controls chain retention and eligibility, rather than outer convergence.

BLX alpha defaults to `0.5`, the mating pool to `4`, mutation probability to
`0.125`, and BGA range fraction to `0.1`. See the
[steady-state GA guide](../../global-and-stochastic/ssga/) for the roles
of those global operators.

### Allow for complete local segments

Set outer execution limits on `Executor`. One outer iteration includes the
GA phase and a local segment, so the outer evaluation allowance can be
exceeded by that work. CMA segments complete whole generations and can
exceed their segment allowance by up to `lambda_inner - 1` evaluations.
Solis–Wets can spend one extra evaluation on a reversal.

CMA chains start at half the neighbor distance and use a per-segment TolX
of `1e-12` times the segment's starting scale. `with_inner_lambda` configures
CMA's population size. Solis–Wets segments use their budget without a
per-segment tolerance. Its published large-scale configuration used an
intensity of `500`; Basin keeps the family default of `300`.

An inner `SolverFailed` propagates to the outer solve. Budget stops and
operator tolerances end a segment cleanly. Enable `with_inner_diagnostics`
when you need stopping reports instead of inferring local convergence from
a returned point.

## Implementation

`PopulationProgress` owns the population and historical incumbent. The
solver owns chains, eligibility history, the operator prototype, and the
seeded outer RNG. Use `Executor::run_with_solver` to inspect `chain(i)` and
`ls_application_count(i)` for the currently sorted population.

Fresh initialization discards chains and resets the outer random stream.
Exact solver-and-state checkpoints retain the chains, population, RNG, and
evaluation counts and skip initialization. With `serde`, serialization
requires the local solver, its state, and the parameters to support it.
A state-only snapshot starts fresh chains.

Both shipped configurations support `Vec<F>`, `nalgebra::DVector<F>`,
`ndarray::Array1<F>`, and `faer::Col<F>`, with `F = f32` or `f64`.
CMA chains additionally require CMA-ES's matrix capabilities; Solis–Wets
chains need only vector operations. A custom `ResumableInner` can narrow
backend support.
